    docs(CF-189): destroying an agent is a measured step, not BLOCKED
    
    docs/OPERATIONS.md told operators, in four places, that destroying a bunker
    agent is unmeasured / BLOCKED / NOT YET and that its CLI verb must not be
    guessed. Both halves are false at this tip:
    
      * scripts/node-create.sh:54 (inside incomplete(), guarded by
        [ "$SPAWNED" -eq 1 ]) prints the exact line
        `cleanup required: bunker destroy <agent> --server <server>` when a
        provisioning run leaves an agent behind - the create path never
        destroys anything itself, it hands the operator that command.
      * docs/audits/code-factory-dogfood-2026-11-04-02-20-15.md section 2c
        records that exact verb being run to completion on 2026-11-04: home
        256.9 MB archived before delete, "Agent ... destroyed.", local SSH key
        removed, rc=0, and bunker list back to the pre-existing agent count.
    
    So the runbook forbade the one lifecycle step its own tooling instructs the
    operator to perform, leaving the reader unable to tell whether following the
    printed command was supported.
    
    Corrected (docs/OPERATIONS.md:63,167,171,175,205): destroy is documented as a
    measured operator step citing the code path and the committed transcript;
    renewing a TTL, rebuilding from a manifest, credential revocation, the
    destroy-then-recreate round trip and every teardown step other than destroy
    stay BLOCKED / NOT YET and are labelled not measured. CF-110's recreate gate
    is unchanged.
    
    Evidence and both probe arms (6 stale claims before, 0 after) are in
    docs/audits/code-factory-docs-2026-11-04-cf189-destroy-prose.md.
    
    Gates at this commit: bash tests/selftest.sh -> SELFTEST: PASS, rc=0
    (193 PASS / 50 intentional-negative FAIL rows, loadavg 13.23);
    bash scripts/boardctl validate -> RESULT: OK (4 pre-existing warnings).
    
